#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "864d4411-8284-525e-8ee2-32477d31b900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{16}\\le x<\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290383} {:answer/id #uuid "df2c3c7b-9d65-54ab-9eef-a9449dfacc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x<\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290384} {:answer/id #uuid "5e628c05-94d9-5a47-aca7-9fc97723ad32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{23}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290385} {:answer/id #uuid "0d6e4cda-cffc-5747-b05b-84c427a5cb57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290386} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b3c92c-0767-57b7-b3ca-e1a0d453eb94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le \\frac{23}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e628c05-94d9-5a47-aca7-9fc97723ad32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{23}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290385} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fff7798-1026-5c26-acd7-89c131212609" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "811d98eb-aeee-5382-8ca0-3caca3e3ff71" :question/math-academy-id "q-102970" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(3-2x)+3\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3-2x)+3 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(3-2x) & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(3-2x)} & \\ge {2}^{-3} \\\\ 3-2x & \\ge \\frac{1}{8} \\\\ -2x & \\ge -\\frac{23}{8} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{23}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n3-2x>0\\,⇒\\,x<\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{23}{16},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}].$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x<\\frac{3}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}]\\cap (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})=(-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{23}{16}],$ which can also be written as $x\\le \\frac{23}{16}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e416d688-2ff5-5afd-ba4e-6a10c7b68df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290390} {:answer/id #uuid "c537262f-0217-57d7-8512-cc5e8abb74ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}<x<\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290391} {:answer/id #uuid "9c63b0c9-4081-5d03-b252-65fc0265ded1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6<x<-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290392} {:answer/id #uuid "0240129f-a945-5d1e-97ea-28a55f8bf313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290393} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a677be-8474-5f2f-b804-7f4f2d7491c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6<x<\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e416d688-2ff5-5afd-ba4e-6a10c7b68df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290390} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9d0d56a-5519-5ab0-9fea-bde847aaef51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31b64f03-5dc6-5a5a-b015-b94acd6a2503" :question/math-academy-id "q-102982" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x+7)>\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-5).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $10.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x+7)} & >{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-5)} \\\\ 4x+7 & >2x-5 \\\\ 2x & >-12 \\\\ x & >-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+7 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ 2x-5 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>-6,$ which can be expressed as $(-6,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-\\frac{7}{4}$ and $x>\\frac{5}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )$ and $(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-6,\\infty )\\cap (-\\frac{7}{4},\\infty )\\cap (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )=(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa09b022-0e31-53c0-b268-2f9ac542a0d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290397} {:answer/id #uuid "78d77802-a8b9-5376-b396-6938c960af5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa09b022-0e31-53c0-b268-2f9ac542a0d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290397} :answer-field/id #uuid "9572af10-673e-5248-b371-a675c032ddc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d782f956-53a9-5c69-814d-cfbcaa8d081c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d782f956-53a9-5c69-814d-cfbcaa8d081c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290400} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2436dff-3b4d-591d-b010-41224a182980" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290399} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b79e197-493f-536b-88b5-d3a573525827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b79e197-493f-536b-88b5-d3a573525827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290402} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d668fc8-7d43-58ed-aa24-e2d3e2738033" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a2aebdd-2ed1-5394-8090-5eb4190b37a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-278508" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $9{log}_{4}⁡(5x-10)-6<3.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$x\\,<$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{log}_{4}⁡(5x-10)-6 & <3 \\\\ 9{log}_{4}⁡(5x-10) & <9 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(5x-10) & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $4.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(5x-10)} & <{4}^{1} \\\\ 5x-10 & <4 \\\\ 5x & <14 \\\\ x & <\\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n5x-10>0\\,⇒\\,x>2\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<\\frac{14}{5},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{14}{5}).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{14}{5})\\cap (2,\\infty )=(2,\\frac{14}{5})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (2,\\frac{14}{5}),$ which can also be written as $2<x<\\frac{14}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9401a0c7-7cd0-5aa5-a3f7-63666ab7f52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9401a0c7-7cd0-5aa5-a3f7-63666ab7f52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290405} :answer-field/id #uuid "d36b71b8-27d9-5c83-b18f-a7e844cf7903" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290404} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da464385-3767-52ac-8bcb-424dc9f2e784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^4}{e^4-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da464385-3767-52ac-8bcb-424dc9f2e784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^4}{e^4-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290407} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f66cdf-edae-5f03-a0dc-b76e20c873cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8ae3519-a626-58b5-9d54-8a75cb13bf97" :question/math-academy-id "q-278674" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)+4\\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x).$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)+4} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}\\cdot {e}^{4} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)} \\\\ (x-1)\\cdot {e}^{4} & \\ge 2x \\\\ x{e}^{4}-{e}^{4} & \\ge 2x \\\\ x{e}^{4}-2x & \\ge {e}^{4} \\\\ x({e}^{4}-2) & \\ge {e}^{4} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >1 \\\\ 2x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>1$ and $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(1,\\infty )$ and $(0,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2},\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=[\\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\nx\\ge \\frac{{e}^{4}}{{e}^{4}-2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010c4e33-71e9-578e-b16f-d921e626eaaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290410} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd3f638-7efb-54e0-911c-24296d97eb6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290411} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0872cc-8688-5480-b567-ddf389a7ad1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290412} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5a766d-fbf1-52f5-9a18-0319386c8461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290413} {:answer/id #uuid "764ab459-b6df-5a06-87f2-222ab169a9f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6<x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bd3f638-7efb-54e0-911c-24296d97eb6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290411} :answer-field/id #uuid "d036221f-dae9-5d74-bb25-c0a3daacb369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "945cc19b-dd63-5c46-b14b-c6f27b95b038" :question/math-academy-id "q-88017" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(x-3)+3>{log}_{2}⁡(4x).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-3)+3} & >{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4x)} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-3)}\\cdot {2}^{3} & >{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4x)} \\\\ (x-3)\\cdot 8 & >4x \\\\ 8x-24 & >4x \\\\ 4x & >24 \\\\ x & >6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >3 \\\\ 4x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>6,$ which can be expressed as $(6,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>3$ and $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(3,\\infty )$ and $(0,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(6,\\infty )\\cap (3,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=(6,\\infty )\n$$\n\n Therefore, the solution is $x\\in (6,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>6.$"}]}
